      "writtenOn": "2020-07-07T17:23:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For Bazel (Java) tests, I\u0027m used to skipping error checking in tests when we have confidence that the error will be empty. Checking for every error scenario seems like it would make the test far more verbose and difficult to see the meat of the test at a glance. Are things typically different for soong (Go) tests? I\u0027ve updated the test at your request, but the new clauses bloat the test IMO.",
      "parentUuid": "976161c1_277dcc62",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 517,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 518,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "9248cd6643cd16d801e4cb84d0b7fa0838e2c0f9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "06012594_d20123db",
        "filename": "cc/cc_test.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 518,
      "author": {
        "id": 1687194
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-07T18:10:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the cast in tests, we often just omit the `, _`, which would reduce the bloat somewhat.\n\nOtherwise, it seems fairly common to check for errors in soong tests. Often it can just be t.Fatal(err).\n\nOther things that may help reduce the verbosity: it\u0027s fairly common to collect errors rather than exit the test early. Or using t.Fatal instead of t.Error to exit the test if the rest of the test cannot continue. In this test, the size checks would be fatal and then you could omit the returns below to collect both errors if necessary.",
      "parentUuid": "fe9a934f_d115f3c7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 517,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 518,
        "endChar": 70
      },
